---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="SWAG BNN for Satellite Image Classification">
  <a href="/" class="back">← Back to projects</a>

  <article class="blog">
    <header>
      <span class="tag">Applications</span>
      <h1>Uncertainty-Aware Land-Use Classification from Satellite Imagery Using SWAG</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        Implementing SWA-Gaussian as an approximate Bayesian neural network for satellite image
        classification — with calibrated confidence and a tuned abstention mechanism that knows when not to guess.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The Problem: Getting Wrong Costs More Than Saying Nothing</h2>
      <p>
        The dataset consists of 60×60 RGB satellite images, each belonging to one of six land-use
        classes. The training set contains 1,800 clean, unambiguous images. The test set is harder:
        some patches contain a mixture of land-use types that don't appear cleanly in training —
        these are treated as <em>ambiguous</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This means the model can't simply classify every image. For each test sample it must either
        output one of the six class labels (0–5) or output <strong>−1</strong>, meaning "don't know".
        Abstaining costs one point. A wrong confident prediction costs three. A model that guesses
        aggressively on uncertain inputs will therefore be penalised far more than one that knows when
        to stay quiet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The overall evaluation criterion combines both concerns:
        <strong>overall cost = mean prediction cost + max(ECE − 0.1, 0)</strong>, where ECE is the
        Expected Calibration Error measured over 20 confidence bins. The calibration penalty only
        activates once ECE exceeds 10%, so a well-calibrated model pays nothing extra on that term.
        Passing requires an overall cost below 0.856.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why a Bayesian Neural Network?</h2>
      <p>
        A standard trained neural network produces a single set of weights and a softmax probability
        vector at inference time. But those probabilities reflect the model's confidence given those
        fixed weights — not genuine uncertainty about whether the image is unambiguously classifiable.
        A network can be highly confident and completely wrong, especially on inputs that look
        different from the training distribution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bayesian neural networks instead maintain a distribution over weights. At inference time,
        predictions are made by averaging over many networks sampled from this posterior — a process
        called <em>Bayesian model averaging</em> (BMA). When sampled networks agree, the averaged
        probabilities are sharp. When they disagree, the average spreads out, naturally producing
        lower confidence. This is exactly the behaviour needed when overconfidence is penalised
        more heavily than abstention.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>SWAG: Mining SGD for an Approximate Posterior</h2>
      <p>
        Exact Bayesian inference over neural network weights is computationally intractable.
        SWA-Gaussian (SWAG) constructs a practical approximation by exploiting a natural property
        of stochastic gradient descent: as SGD runs near a local minimum, the iterates trace out a
        region of weight space that implicitly reflects posterior uncertainty.
      </p>
      <p>
        Starting from pretrained MAP weights, continued SGD runs for 30 additional epochs at a fixed
        learning rate of 0.045. At the end of each epoch, two running statistics are updated for
        every network parameter: the running mean of the weights (θ̄) and the running mean of their
        squares. These two moments define a <em>diagonal Gaussian</em> approximation to the
        posterior — the per-parameter variance is the mean of squares minus the square of the mean.
        This is SWAG-diagonal.
      </p>
      <p>
        Full SWAG extends this with a low-rank covariance term. After each epoch, the difference
        between the current weights and the running mean is stored in a capped deque of depth 15.
        These stored deviations capture correlated directions along which SGD moved, letting the
        posterior model covariances between parameters rather than treating each weight independently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Sampling and Bayesian Model Averaging</h2>
      <p>
        To draw a sample from the approximate posterior, a new set of weights is constructed as:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>θ = θ̄ + (1/√2) · σ_diag ⊙ z₁ + (1/√(2(K−1))) · D · z₂</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        where θ̄ is the SWA running mean, σ_diag is the per-parameter standard deviation from the
        diagonal approximation, D is the deviation matrix (shape K × d, where K is the deque depth
        and d is the number of parameters in that layer), z₁ is standard normal noise matching the
        parameter shape, and z₂ is a K-dimensional standard normal vector. The D · z₂ term is
        flattened and reshaped to match each layer before adding.
      </p>
      <p>
        After sampling, batch normalisation statistics must be recomputed. Batch norm running means
        and variances were fitted alongside the original MAP weights; a freshly sampled weight vector
        produces different activation distributions and stale statistics would corrupt predictions.
        A full forward pass over the training set with the sampled weights resets these statistics
        before inference runs. This step is repeated for each of the 30 BMA samples, and the final
        predictive distribution is the elementwise mean of all 30 softmax output vectors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Abstention: One Threshold Per Class, Not One for All</h2>
      <p>
        Once BMA probabilities are in hand, the final step is deciding whether to output a class
        label or −1. The six land-use classes are not equally easy to classify confidently — some
        have distinctive visual signatures while others are frequently confused with their neighbours.
        A single global threshold applies the same stringency everywhere, but what we really want is
        for each class to abstain at the rate that minimises its contribution to total cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        The label prediction step uses a <em>per-class threshold vector</em> instead. Classes prone
        to overconfident wrong predictions get a higher threshold. Class 3 — which appears most often
        in ambiguous test images — is set to a threshold of 0, meaning the model always predicts it
        when it is the argmax, since abstaining on a genuinely ambiguous image still costs one point.
      </p>
      <p>
        Formally, the prediction for sample i is: argmax(p_i) if max(p_i) ≥ threshold[argmax(p_i)],
        else −1. This lets the abstention boundary adapt to each class's characteristic confidence
        distribution rather than imposing a one-size-fits-all rule.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the Model Gets Right — and Where It Struggles</h2>
      <p>
        The BMA procedure produces noticeably wider predictive distributions on ambiguous images
        compared to MAP inference alone. When the 30 sampled networks disagree about a patch that
        mixes land-use types, the averaged probabilities are spread across multiple classes rather
        than concentrating on one — the model's uncertainty naturally triggers abstention without
        any extra mechanism.
      </p>
      <p>
        The per-class thresholds were tuned on a validation set of just 140 images — 20 per class
        plus 20 ambiguous. With so few examples per class, the threshold optimum found on validation
        may not generalise perfectly to the held-out test distribution. A more principled approach
        would be temperature scaling: learning a single scalar T that divides all logits before
        softmax, recalibrating the confidence surface while reducing the number of tuned parameters
        from six to one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deviation matrix is populated from one snapshot per epoch. With 30 SWAG epochs and a
        deque depth of 15, at most the last 15 directions of weight evolution are captured. The
        posterior approximation is therefore limited to the most recent region explored by SGD,
        rather than representing the full loss basin geometry.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I'd Do Differently</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Replace the per-class threshold vector with temperature scaling fitted to the validation set — fewer parameters to overfit and a more principled recalibration of the full softmax surface.</li>
        <li>Use a cyclical learning rate schedule during the SWAG phase to push weights into different parts of the loss basin, producing more diverse samples and a richer covariance approximation without additional training epochs.</li>
        <li>Increase snapshot frequency beyond once per epoch — more frequent updates to the deviation matrix would let the rank-15 deque cover a wider slice of the SGD trajectory.</li>
        <li>Experiment with more BMA samples; 30 networks is enough for stable estimates on this dataset, but confidence in rare or borderline classes would benefit from a larger ensemble.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>

  <section id="resources" class="resources">
